# ↪️ Pull Request

This tries to fix the second part of #4524.

It looks like dependencies that are only used in a dead branch after the process visitor was executed are still included in the bundle.

By removing the dead branches directly this should be solved.

Closes #4524

@mischnic I am not 100% sure if this is the solution to the issue. First, the test is not passing. Not sure if I just took the wrong approach to test it.

But also, when running parcel with or without the removed dead branches on the example in the test, it results in the same code: It never contains code for dependency `a.js` (which is the wanted behavior anyhow).

But why is the test failing? Did I miss something? Do we need to have another approach? And is this an issue at all? Maybe you have some ideas on that :)

## 💻 Examples

``` javascript
import { act } from 'react-dom/test-utils'
const fireAsAct = (cb: () => void) => {
  if (process.env.NODE_ENV === "test") {
    act(() => cb());
  } else {
    cb();
  }
};

fireAsAct(() => console.log('test'));
```

## ✔️ PR Todo

- [ ] Find out why the tests are not passing for the dead code check
- [ ] Discuss if this is actually the solution to the original issue
- [ ] Maybe find some better solutions to remove the branches

